**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapters 3, 6, and 7 with the complete rock review; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), the complete Rock Identification lab and charts; [Laboratory Manual for Introductory Geology — Deline, Harris, and Tefend](../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z), the complete corresponding rock labs.

A rock classification organizes observations for a purpose. Record grain/mineral relationships, texture, composition, structures, weathering/alteration, and field context before assigning a name. A hand specimen, thin section, outcrop, and mapped body provide different kinds and scales of information.

Texture can constrain crystallization, deposition, deformation, or recrystallization, but several processes can overprint one specimen. A coarse texture alone does not supply a unique cooling rate or depth; layering can be sedimentary, igneous, metamorphic, or composite. Use specific relationships and independent evidence to support a history.

The rock cycle describes possible transfers and transformations rather than a mandatory loop every specimen completes. Preserve protolith, transport, reaction, melting/crystallization, deformation, and exposure as separately testable parts of a proposed path. A specimen's present classification need not reveal every earlier event.

**Work:** make original descriptions of igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic teaching examples using the full lab charts. Explain the classification and identify one historical inference requiring field evidence beyond the specimen. Compare a fresh and an altered description, noting which features support each conclusion.

Read the complete rock chapters and their review before the deeper process sequence. This general foundation should support field mapping and source criticism, while specialist petrology will develop quantitative phase/texture methods and their physical/chemical models.

**Continue:** [Explain igneous materials through melting, crystallization, and field relations](Melting-crystallization-and-igneous-bodies.lingua#r_7QWRZH8D2EVJR6F20Z77Y4QXR7).

